  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A moderate (G2) to strong (G3) geomagnetic storm is expected to impact Earth through March 20–21, 2026, due to multiple Coronal Mass Ejections (CMEs). This solar activity, following a high peak in the 11-year cycle, may cause aurora displays further south, potentially in northern US states. Potential impacts include minor satellite issues and radio disruptions. Key Aspects of Geomagnetic Storms Causes: These disturbances are caused by solar winds, solar flares, and Coronal Mass Ejections (CMEs), which are massive explosions of plasma and magnetic fields from the Sun. Impact on Earth: When charged particles hit Earth's magnetosphere, they can create stunning auroras. Technological Risks: Strong storms can damage satellites, disrupt GPS, and cause power grid failures, with severe storms capable of causing widespread blackouts. Classification: NOAA classifies them on a G-scale from G1 (minor) to G5 (extreme). Historical Events: The 1859 Carrington Event is the most intense on record, disrupting telegraph systems. In 1989, a storm caused a major power blackout in Quebec, Canada. Current Solar Activity (March 2026) Spring Equinox Effect: The weeks around the March 20 equinox are often \"aurora season\" due to enhanced interaction between solar wind and Earth's magnetic field. Active Period: The Sun is currently in a high-activity phase of its 11-year cycle, which is projected to continue through 2027. Recent Events: A G4 (severe) storm was recorded on January 19, 2026, following an X-class solar flare. For real-time updates and forecasts, visit the NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center (SWPC). NOAA Space Weather Prediction Center (SWPC)",
